<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>vectors</title></head><body><h1>vectors</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors</li><li>Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication</li><li>Dot Product and Its Applications</li><li>Dot Product of Two Vectors</li><li>Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent and ind…</li><li>Linear Combination of Vectors</li><li>Cross Product and Its Applications</li><li>Cross Product of Two Vectors</li><li>Scalar and Vector Triple Products</li><li>Applications of Vectors â€” Work, Torque and Angular Momentum</li></ul><h2>Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Dot Product and Its Applications</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Dot Product and Its Applications</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Dot Product and Its Applications to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Dot Product and Its Applications covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Dot Product of Two Vectors</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>In component form the dot product is just the sum of products — the angle only appears if the question gives magnitudes.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Dot Product of Two Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent and ind…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent and ind… — Vectors, Mathematics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent, independent vectors · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Vectors have magnitude + direction; a = aî + bĵ + c k̂. |a| = √(a²+b²+c²).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Dot: a·b = |a||b|cosθ = a₁b₁+a₂b₂+a₃b₃ (scalar; =0 ⟂). Cross: a×b (vector, ⟂ to both; |a×b| = |a||b|sinθ; area = ½|a×b|).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Triangle/parallelogram/polygon laws: vector sum is independent of order (commutative).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Linearly dependent.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Independent vectors.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Linear combination of vectors</td></tr><tr><td>• Linearly dependent</td></tr><tr><td>• Independent vectors</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Linear combination of vectors" as one blob → ✅ split into: linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent, independent vectors.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ answer with no steps → ✅ show each step and box the result.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ vectors have magnitude + direction; a = aî + bĵ + c k̂. |a| = √(a²+b²+c²)..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent and ind… (Vectors, Mathematics, class-11-notes) covers Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent, independent vectors. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Linear Combination of Vectors</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Linear Combination of Vectors</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Linear Combination of Vectors to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Linear Combination of Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Cross Product and Its Applications</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Cross Product and Its Applications</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Cross Product and Its Applications to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Cross Product and Its Applications covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Cross Product of Two Vectors</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Area of a triangle on two vectors is ½|a×b| — the parallelogram area with the half that students forget.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Cross Product of Two Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Scalar and Vector Triple Products</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Coplanarity test in one line: the volume [a b c] is 0 — use it instead of solving three planes for a common point.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Scalar and Vector Triple Products covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Applications of Vectors â€” Work, Torque and Angular Momentum</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Work.** Work done by a constant force is the dot product W = F·s = Fs cosθ; it is maximum when force and displacement are parallel and zero when they are perpendicular.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Torque.** The moment of a force about a point is τ = r × F, with magnitude rF sinθ and direction given by the right-hand rule; torque measures the turning effect.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Angular momentum.** The angular momentum of a particle is L = r × p = r × mv, and for a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis L = Iω.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Newton's law for rotation.** The net external torque equals the rate of change of angular momentum, τ = dL/dt, in exact analogy with F = dp/dt.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Conservation.** If the net external torque on a system is zero, its angular momentum stays constant — a spinning skater speeds up when pulling in their arms.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Applications of Vectors â€” Work, Torque and Angular Momentum to other topics</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Applications of Vectors — Work, Torque, Angular Momentum to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Applications of Vectors â€” Work, Torque and Angular Momentum covers essential principles and applications. Applications of Vectors — Work, Torque, Angular Momentum covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors</td><td>Collinear and Non-collinear Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication</td><td>Vector Algebra — Types, Addition and Scalar Multiplication covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Dot Product and Its Applications</td><td>Dot Product and Its Applications covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Dot Product of Two Vectors</td><td>Dot Product of Two Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent and ind…</td><td>Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent and ind… (Vectors, Mathematics, class-11-notes) covers Linear combination of vectors, linearly dependent, independent vectors. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Linear Combination of Vectors</td><td>Linear Combination of Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Cross Product and Its Applications</td><td>Cross Product and Its Applications covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Cross Product of Two Vectors</td><td>Cross Product of Two Vectors covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Scalar and Vector Triple Products</td><td>Scalar and Vector Triple Products covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Applications of Vectors â€” Work, Torque and Angular Momentum</td><td>Applications of Vectors â€” Work, Torque and Angular Momentum covers essential principles and applications. Applications of Vectors — Work, Torque, Angular Momentum covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table></body></html>